Switch REJECT on when a grammar spells it yyreject()

A grammar that uses flex's own back-end-neutral spelling gets a scanner
that does not compile:

    %option noyywrap
    %%
    "ab"    { yyreject(); }
    "a"     { printf("a\n"); }
    [a-z]   { printf("one\n"); }

    $ flex --emit=c99 -o scan.c reject.l && cc -c scan.c
    error: no member named 'yy_full_match' in 'struct yyguts_t'
    error: no member named 'yy_lp' in 'struct yyguts_t'

The REJECT expansion is emitted but the scanner has none of the state it
needs, because `reject' was never set. scan.l's arm matches the call
including its parentheses:

    "yyreject"{OPTWS}"("{OPTWS}")" {
            add_action("]""]M4_HOOK_REJECT[""[");
            CHECK_YYREJECT(yytext);
    }

and CHECK_YYREJECT tests all_lower(), which any parenthesis turns down.
So the branch never fires. Writing REJECT instead of yyreject() in the
same grammar emits the state and compiles.

Passing the name rather than the match is enough.

Applies to flex master as of 4fcc714. --emit=c99 and --emit=go are both
affected; the C/C++ skeleton is too, in that yyreject() there has the
same silent effect.
---
diff --git a/src/scan.l b/src/scan.l
index b65bd4c..6b41251 100644
--- a/src/scan.l
+++ b/src/scan.l
@@ -925,7 +925,10 @@ nmstr[yyleng - 2 - end_is_ws] = '\0';  /* chop trailing brace */
         }
 		"yyreject"{OPTWS}"("{OPTWS}")" {
 			add_action("]""]M4_HOOK_REJECT[""[");
-			CHECK_YYREJECT(yytext);
+			/* The name, not the match: yytext carries the parentheses
+			   too, and all_lower turns them down, so REJECT was never
+			   switched on for a grammar that spells it yyreject(). */
+			CHECK_YYREJECT("yyreject");
         }
 		"yymore"{OPTWS}"("{OPTWS}")" {
 			yymore_used = true;
